\section{Variance}{

The ratio is linearised. Its variance is that of the Horvitz-Thompson
total of the residuals \verb{(y - R x) / X_hat}, with \code{X_hat = sum(x / pi)}
(Särndal, Swensson and Wretman 1992; Deville 1999), and is computed with
the design's own estimator, as for a total: see "Variance" and
"Confidence intervals" in \code{\link[=ht_total]{ht_total()}} for the estimators, the fallback
to the jackknife, the degrees of freedom and the notes. For the designs
\code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} hands to \code{survey} with identical standard errors, the
standard error equals that of \code{survey::svyratio()} on its result. For
systematic, spread and systematic PPS samples it comes from the
estimator this package uses for their totals, which \code{survey} does not
have.

The residuals sum to zero over the sample, weighted by \code{1 / pi}, so a
ratio whose sampled rows lie in one primary sampling unit, one row or one
cluster, gets no standard error, as a Hajek mean does. When \code{y} is
proportional to \code{x} in every sampled row the residuals are 0, the
variance is set to exactly 0 rather than left to rounding error, and
\code{note} says so.

The ratio of two estimated totals is biased, by an amount of order
\code{1 / n}, which is small against its standard error except in small
samples. The design effect compares its variance with that of the same
ratio from a simple random sample of the same size, as
\code{survey::svyratio(deff = TRUE)} does.
}

\section{Domains}{

\code{by} gives a ratio for each domain, \code{R_d = Y_d / X_d} over the rows of
the domain, estimated from the whole sample. Each domain's residuals,
\verb{(y - R_d x) / X_hat_d}, are 0 outside it, as for the domain totals and
means described under "Domains" in \code{\link[=ht_total]{ht_total()}}, and the standard errors
are those of
\code{survey::svyby(~y, ~d, design, survey::svyratio, denominator = ~x)}. A
domain whose estimated total of \code{x} is 0 is refused by name.

A missing \code{y} or \code{x} is refused. \code{survey::svyratio(na.rm = TRUE)} on
\code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} estimates the ratio over the rows that have both.
}

